## 2. Binding Decisions and Standards

| ID | The rule, in one line | What it governs here |
|----|------------------------|----------------------|
| `decisions-001-dumb-infrastructure` | Lore stores and answers queries; it never interprets, orchestrates, or runs a template engine — and "minimise tool calls" is a design constraint, not an aspiration. | `doctrine.show_doctrine`/`read_doctrine` must not parse or execute mission-file prose; `lore doctrine show` design+index-in-one-call is this principle applied. |
| `decisions-003-soft-delete-semantics` | File entities (knights, doctrines) are soft-deleted by renaming with a `.deleted` suffix, never hard-deleted; this ADR's scope explicitly excludes Lore's *own* source-code changes (removing `knight.py` is ordinary hard-delete, not soft-delete ceremony). | `--remove-mission` on `lore doctrine edit` must soft-delete `missions/<id>.md` → `missions/<id>.md.deleted` (FR-11), not unlink it. Deleting `src/lore/knight.py` itself is plain code removal — not governed by this ADR. |
| `decisions-004-mission-type-dumb-storage` | `mission_type` is stored and exposed, never interpreted; no enum, no CHECK constraint, no CLI `click.Choice`. | The PRD's "`knight`→`agent`" token swap (FR-20) is a seeded-content/skill convention change only — **no code path enforces this**, confirmed by `migrations/v4_to_v5.py` already stripping the old enum/CHECK/DEFAULT. |
| `decisions-006-id-references` | Agents reference entities by ID through the CLI, never by constructed file path; the by-ID rule (updated 2026-08-25) currently enumerates exactly four file-backed categories that keep it in *both* access modes: "Artifacts, knights, doctrines, watchers." | Removing Knight as an entity leaves this ADR's own text naming a category that no longer exists — see Conflict #1. |
| `decisions-006-no-seed-content-tests` | Tests must never assert specific field *values* from `src/lore/defaults/` seed files — only existence, parseability, and structural-key presence are valid targets. | Governs every new/edited test touching `src/lore/defaults/doctrines/`, `defaults/knights/` (deleted), `defaults/artifacts/lore-design-documents/mission-design.md`, `defaults/skills-catalogue.yaml`. |
| `decisions-008-help-as-teaching-interface` | Top-level `lore --help` and command-group `--help` must teach the entity/workflow model, not just syntax; individual subcommand/flag help stays terse. | `lore --help`'s conceptual paragraph currently names "quests, missions, knights, doctrines" — needs updating to six (not seven) entities; `lore doctrine --help` group paragraph needs the new mission-file model explained. |
| `decisions-010-public-api-stability` | `lore.api.__all__` is the sole public API surface; **removing** a name requires a major bump or an explicit `CHANGELOG.md` breaking-change notice. | Removing `Knight`, `list_knights`, `read_knight`, `create_knight`, `update_knight`, `delete_knight` from `lore.api.__all__` (FR-17) is a breaking change under this ADR's own semver table — the PRD names no version/changelog plan. |
| `decisions-011-api-parity-with-cli` | Every `lore.api` function must be self-contained and behaviourally identical to the CLI; no validation or behaviour may live only in `cli.py`. | `create_doctrine`/`update_doctrine`'s new multi-mission validation (atomic, ≥1 mission, no duplicate stems) must live in `doctrine.py`, not `cli.py`. See Conflict #2 for a place this contract is currently under-specified. |
| `decisions-012-multi-value-cli-param-convention` | Multi-value CLI flags are space-separated (`--flag a b`), never repeatable (`--flag a --flag b`); `-m`/`--remove-mission` on `doctrine new`/`edit` must follow this. | `lore doctrine new -m file1.md file2.md file3.md` and `lore doctrine edit --remove-mission a b` (both FR-9/FR-10) — confirmed space-separated form is what the PRD's own examples already use. |
| `decisions-017-constrained-flags-use-click-choice` | Constrained-value flags use `click.Choice`; adding a valid token is non-breaking, but the enforcement mechanism/wording/exit-code (2) is a pinned contract, amendable only in place. | `lore health --scope` must drop `"knights"` from its `click.Choice` set (FR-19) — a straightforward token removal, not a mechanism change, so no ADR amendment is triggered by this alone. |
| `decisions-024-default-group-is-seeded-boundary` | An entity is a seeded default iff its group is `default` or starts with `default/`; `.lore/.install-manifest.json` is never consulted for this test — file-backed entities only. | Governs `reconcile`'s removal of `.lore/knights/default/**` and reseed of `.lore/doctrines/default/**` in the new shape (FR-24) — the *doctrine* directory nesting one level deeper (`default/<doctrine-id>/missions/*.md`) does not change the `is_seeded_default` test, since it only inspects the derived `group` string. |
| `decisions-025-cross-boundary-reads-are-read-only` | `projects.reject_foreign` is the first statement of every externally callable write function on a file-backed entity; `--project` is rejected at exit 2 on every write command. | `create_doctrine`/`update_doctrine`/`delete_doctrine` already call this (confirmed in `tech-arch-source-layout`'s module table) — the new multi-file write path must keep calling it first, before any mission-file validation. |
| `standards-dependency-inversion` | `doctrine.py`/`knight.py` may import `paths`, `frontmatter`, `validators` only — never `cli.py` or `db.py`. | The rewritten `doctrine.py` (directory model) must not gain a `cli.py`/`db.py` import; `knight.py`'s deletion removes one compliant module, not a violation. |
| `standards-dry` | Doctrine/knight loading and validation each have exactly one authoritative module home; shape validation lives in JSON Schema, never inline. | The new `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` schema must carry `id`/`title`/`summary` shape rules — cross-field checks (mission `id` == filename stem) stay inline in `doctrine.py`, mirroring today's `_validate_yaml_schema` split. |
| `standards-facade` | `lore.api` is a pure re-export facade — zero `def`/`class`; new names are added via the operational-callables block. | `create_doctrine`/`update_doctrine`'s new signatures and any new `read_doctrine(..., mission=...)` path re-export cleanly; Knight removal deletes six re-exports and their underscore alias `_knight`. |
| `standards-public-api-stability` | Same semver table as ADR-010, restated as a standards doc — removing a name from `__all__` is major-bump-or-notice. | Reinforces the Conflict #2/gap above: the PRD's Success Criteria table says "Entity types in Lore: 7 → 6" but assigns no version/changelog plan for the removal. |
| `standards-separation-of-concerns` | `cli.py` formats and exits; business logic (validation, atomicity, duplicate detection) lives in `db.py`/entity modules. | The new `lore doctrine new -d -m` / `--remove-mission` CLI handlers must stay thin — all directory-write logic goes in `doctrine.py`. |
| `standards-single-responsibility` | Each module owns exactly one concern; `knight.py`/`doctrine.py` each handle one file-based entity type. | Deleting `knight.py` outright (not folding its logic into `doctrine.py`) is the compliant shape — mission-file logic belongs inside `doctrine.py`, which already owns the doctrine directory. |

### Apparent Conflicts

| Rule id | What the request seems to want | Why that reads as a conflict |
|---------|-------------------------------|------------------------------|
| `decisions-006-id-references` | Remove the Knight entity outright, with no compatibility shim. | ADR-006's own Decision and Consequences sections *currently* name "Knights" as one of exactly four entity categories bound by the by-ID-through-CLI rule ("Artifacts, knights, doctrines, watchers — by ID through the CLI"), and this exact sentence was reaffirmed in the ADR's most recent (2026-08-25) status-history revision. The PRD scopes no ADR-006 edit. Left alone, a settled, recently-touched decision keeps describing an entity that no longer exists. |
| `decisions-011-api-parity-with-cli` | `lore doctrine show <id> --mission <id>` on a missing mission must report a CLI error distinguishing "doctrine exists, mission missing" (`Mission "recon" not found in doctrine "tdd-feature-lite"`, exit 1) from a missing doctrine. | The PRD's own Python-surface line for the *other* workflow ("Read a doctrine to plan a quest") states `read_doctrine(..., mission="red")` "returns the dict with an additional `mission` key … **or `None` when that mission does not exist**" — parallel to "returns that same dict, or `None` on a miss" for a missing doctrine. If a missing mission collapses `read_doctrine` to bare `None`, a caller cannot tell "no such doctrine" from "doctrine exists, no such mission" — the exact distinction the CLI-facing error text requires. ADR-011 requires the Python surface to carry everything the CLI needs; as written, this one doesn't. |
| `decisions-011-api-parity-with-cli` (via the existing `frontmatter_edit._KINDS["doctrine"]` entry) | `lore doctrine edit` becomes `-d` / `-m` / `--remove-mission` only. | `src/lore/frontmatter_edit.py:78-91` today registers **two** kinds off the current two-file model: `"knight"` (removed cleanly by this feature) and `"doctrine"` (`schema_kind="doctrine-yaml"`, `shape="yaml"`, `locator=_doctrine_locator` — resolves `<name>.yaml`). `lore doctrine edit --set/--unset/--add/--remove` field-edit mode is *live today* against that YAML file (confirmed in `cli.py`'s current `doctrine_edit` handler). Once the YAML steps file is deleted, this kind's locator resolves to nothing. The PRD names only "No `doctrine-mission` kind in frontmatter_edit" as out of scope — it never says whether the pre-existing `"doctrine"` kind is removed outright or repointed at `design.md`'s frontmatter (`id`/`title`/`summary`). Left unresolved, `lore doctrine edit --set title=x` either silently breaks or needs a locator rewrite the PRD doesn't authorize. |

_A fourth, non-ADR item — the "mission" naming collision — is named plainly in §6 rather than here, per the recon brief: it is a vocabulary observation, not a decision conflict, and is explicitly not a candidate for a glossary entry._

---

## 3. Code Surface

Real paths, verified against the working tree (`git rev-parse HEAD` at recon time: branch `feat/doctrine-refactor`).

| Path | Lane | What is there now | Expected change |
|------|------|-------------------|------------------|
| `src/lore/doctrine.py` | core | Two-file model: `list_doctrines`/`show_doctrine`/`create_doctrine`/`update_doctrine`/`delete_doctrine`; `_validate_yaml_schema`, `_validate_design_frontmatter`, `_validate_steps`, `_check_cycles` (3-colour DFS), `_normalize`. Writes are plain `Path.write_text()` (line 396) / `mkdir` (line 161) — **not** routed through `lore.safewrite`. | Rewritten wholesale: directory discovery, `missions/` subdir CRUD, atomic multi-file write, mission add/replace/remove, no step/cycle logic. |
| `src/lore/knight.py` | core | `create_knight`, `list_knights`, `_find_knight` (strict traversal guard), `_knight_ref_stem`, `_resolve_knight_ref` (permissive, group-qualified — the fix for the now-stale `preexisting-bugs-handoff` bug #1). | Deleted outright (FR-16–18). |
| `src/lore/cli.py` | surface | `knight_list/show/new/edit/delete` (lines 1633–1994, 5 commands); `doctrine_list/show/new/edit/delete` (lines 1995–2260+, 5 commands) — `doctrine_edit` has both field-edit mode (`--set/--unset/--add/--remove` → `frontmatter_edit`) and whole-file replace mode (`-f`). `lore health --scope` `click.Choice` list includes `"knights"`. | `knight` command group removed; `doctrine new/edit/show` rewritten for `-d`/`-m`/`--remove-mission`/`--mission`; `--scope` Choice drops `"knights"`. |
| `src/lore/api.py` | surface | Pure re-export facade; `Knight`, `list_knights`, `read_knight`, `create_knight`, `update_knight`, `delete_knight` re-exported; `_knight` underscore alias for `cli.py`. | Six knight-related names removed (breaking, see Conflict table); doctrine CRUD signatures updated; possible new `mission=` keyword on `read_doctrine`. |
| `src/lore/db.py` | core | `missions` table access via `create_mission`/`update_mission`/`get_mission_detail` etc., all reading/writing the `knight` column. `db.get_mission_detail` lazily imports `lore.knight.read_knight` for envelope hydration (the documented dependency-inversion exception in `standards-dependency-inversion`). | `knight` param/column becomes `doctrine_mission`; hydration import moves from `lore.knight` to `lore.doctrine`. |
| `src/lore/models.py` | core | `Doctrine` (`id,title,summary,steps: tuple[DoctrineStep,...]`), `DoctrineStep` (`id,title,priority,type,knight,notes,needs`), `Knight` (`name,content`), `Mission` includes `knight: str \| None`. | `DoctrineStep`/step-graph types removed; `Doctrine` gains a mission-index shape; `Knight` deleted; `Mission.knight` → `Mission.doctrine_mission`. |
| `src/lore/health.py` | core | `_check_doctrines` (orphaned-pair, broken-knight-ref-in-step, broken-artifact-ref-in-step-notes), `_check_knights` (missing-file on active missions, via `_resolve_knight_ref`/`_knight_ref_stem` — the resolver-pattern precedent), `_check_schemas` (per-kind dispatch table includes `knight`, `doctrine-yaml`, `doctrine-design-frontmatter`). | `_check_knights` deleted; `_check_doctrines` grows old-shape warnings (`missing_missions_dir`, `stray_yaml`) and an active-mission `doctrine_mission` unresolved-reference **error** at the same severity `_check_knights` used — **should reuse the dual-resolver pattern** (strict resolver for user input vs. permissive resolver for a stored, doctrine-qualified reference) rather than re-deriving it. |
| `src/lore/frontmatter_edit.py` | core | `_KINDS` dict (lines 77–91 confirmed) has a `"knight"` entry (`locator=_knight_mod._find_knight`) **and** a `"doctrine"` entry (`schema_kind="doctrine-yaml"`, `shape="yaml"`, `locator=_doctrine_locator` — resolves `<name>.yaml`). Imports `from lore import knight as _knight_mod`. | `"knight"` entry + its import removed cleanly. `"doctrine"` entry's fate is the open question in Conflict #2 above — **not addressed by the PRD**. |
| `src/lore/paths.py` | foundation | `knights_dir(root)`, `doctrines_dir(root)`, `derive_group`; no `doctrine_missions_dir`-style helper. | `knights_dir` removed; a mission-subdirectory path helper is plausible (e.g. resolving `<doctrine>/missions/<id>.md`) but not required if `doctrine.py` builds the path inline as it does today for `.yaml`/`.design.md`. |
| `src/lore/safewrite.py` | foundation | `atomic_write_bytes`/`atomic_write_text` (temp-file + `os.replace`, symlink/escape refusal). Used today **only** by `init.py`, `manifest.py`, `reconcile.py` — confirmed by grep: no entity module (`doctrine.py`, `knight.py`, `rite.py`, `artifact.py`, `watcher.py`) uses it; all use plain `Path.write_text()`. | The PRD's "Lore writes the whole directory atomically — no partial doctrine is ever left on disk" (FR-9, Reliability NFR) is a **new** guarantee even for the *design.md* — today's two-file `create_doctrine` writes YAML then design.md sequentially with plain `write_text`, with no atomicity between the two. Achieving the PRD's guarantee for N+1 files (design + missions) either extends `safewrite` into an entity module for the first time, or accepts the same non-atomic sequential-write pattern the PRD's own wording rules out. Not previously used this way — Unknown, flagged in §5. |
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-yaml.yaml` | foundation | Top-level `id`/`steps` schema, step shape, knight-conditional (`if type==knight then require knight`). | Deleted — no more machine-readable step graph. |
| `src/lore/schemas/doctrine-design-frontmatter.yaml` | foundation | Required `id`; optional `title`/`summary`; `additionalProperties: false`. | Likely unchanged in shape (doctrine design frontmatter is still `id`/`title`/`summary`) — confirm against FR-3's wording, which describes the *mission* file's frontmatter contract, not the design file's. |
| `src/lore/schemas/knight-frontmatter.yaml` | foundation | Required `id`,`title`,`summary`; `additionalProperties: false`. | Deleted; a new `doctrine-mission-frontmatter.yaml` schema is added with the same shape (FR-3), most likely a near-identical copy. |
| `src/lore/schemas/__init__.py` | foundation | `_SCHEMA_KINDS`-equivalent dispatch (kind → glob/shape) includes `knight`, `doctrine-yaml`, `doctrine-design-frontmatter`. | `knight` and `doctrine-yaml` kinds removed; `doctrine-mission-frontmatter` kind added, source pattern `.lore/doctrines/**/missions/*.md` (frontmatter shape). |
| `src/lore/migrations/` | core | 5 files, `v1_to_v2` … `v5_to_v6`; `SCHEMA_VERSION = 6` (`db.py:13`, confirmed). `v4_to_v5.py` is the direct precedent for "drop a CHECK/enum, rewrite values" work. | New `v6_to_v7.py`: drop `missions.knight`, add `missions.doctrine_mission TEXT`, rewrite `mission_type = 'knight'` rows to `'agent'`. `SCHEMA_VERSION` → 7. |
| `src/lore/defaults/schema.sql` | core (seeded, but structural) | `missions.knight TEXT` column (line 28, confirmed). | `knight` column removed, `doctrine_mission TEXT` added — fresh-DB schema must match the migration's end state. |
| `src/lore/defaults/doctrines/` | scribe | 4 doctrines confirmed on disk: `update-changelog` (root pair) + `feature-implementation/{feature-implementation, quick-feature-implementation, tdd-implementation}` — each still today's `.yaml`+`.design.md` pair. | All 4 converted to the new directory shape; each former knight persona merged into the mission file that used it (FR-27). |
| `src/lore/defaults/knights/` | scribe | 12 files confirmed: `changelog-scribe.md` (root) + 11 under `feature-implementation/`. | Directory removed entirely — content is folded into the corresponding doctrine's mission files, not preserved as a tree. |
| `src/lore/defaults/artifacts/lore-design-documents/` | scribe | 5 files confirmed: `glossary-design.md`, `inquest-design.md`, `rite-design.md`, `codex-voice.md`, `doctrine-design.md`. | `doctrine-design.md` rewritten (no Knight column, `agent\|constable\|human` types, Missions section) (FR-26); new `mission-design.md` added (FR-25) — 2 of what becomes 6 files touched, matching the mission brief's "2 artifacts" figure. |
| `src/lore/defaults/skills/` | scribe | 10 directories confirmed (`store-memory`, `retrieve-memory`, `update-doctrine`, `update-knight`, `update-watcher`, `update-artifact`, `update-custom-schema`, `start-quest`, `inquest`, `sync-codex-guide`). Machinery family currently has 5 skills. | `update-knight/` deleted; `update-doctrine`, `start-quest`, `inquest`, `sync-codex-guide` updated for the new shape (FR-29) — 5 of 10 total skills touched, matching the mission brief's "5 skills" figure; machinery family drops to 4 (FR-28). |
| `src/lore/defaults/skills-catalogue.yaml` | scribe | `version: 2`; `families: {memory, machinery, workflow}`; `update-knight` listed under `machinery`; `retired:` ledger is append-only, 13 entries confirmed, none yet naming `update-knight`. | `update-knight` moves from `skills:` into `retired:` (`{into: update-doctrine, reason: ...}`) per the append-only ledger convention — this is itself the pattern, not a new one. |
| `src/lore/defaults/gitignore` | foundation/scribe (undeclared in the mission brief's known-surface list — found by reading the actual template) | Confirmed on disk: `!knights` / `!knights/**` / `knights/default/` lines (lines 12–14 of the file) alongside the equivalent `doctrines`/`artifacts`/`watchers` triads. | The three `knights`-related lines must be removed; the `doctrines` triad is unaffected (a directory-of-directories under `doctrines/default/` is still covered by the same glob, since `is_seeded_default` only inspects the derived group string, not depth). |
| `src/lore/defaults/docs/LORE-AGENT.md` | scribe | Dispatch-loop text explicitly says: "Type `knight` → claim and spawn a worker agent...". Rendered via the same `skills.render()` access-mode block mechanism as skills. | Updated to name `agent` instead of `knight` and to describe six entity types (FR-30). |
| `src/lore/defaults/docs/GETTING-STARTED.md` | scribe | Copied verbatim, no generated regions; likely mentions Knight/Doctrine in its orientation walkthrough. | Updated for the new entity count/shape (FR-30 names both docs explicitly). |
| `tests/` (85 files reference "knight", grep-verified) | mixed (foundation/core/surface per module under test) | Grep-verified occurrence counts range from 1 (incidental, e.g. a `--knight` flag mentioned once in a broader fixture) to 252 (`tests/e2e/test_knight_list.py`, dedicated). | **8 files are knight-only / wholly deletable**: `tests/e2e/test_knight_list.py`, `tests/e2e/test_knight_crud.py`, `tests/e2e/test_api_parity_knight.py`, `tests/e2e/test_health_grouped_knight_refs.py`, `tests/unit/test_knight_crud.py`, `tests/unit/test_knight.py`, `tests/unit/test_knight_crud_holistic.py`, `tests/unit/test_health_grouped_knight_refs.py`. **The remaining 77 are knight-touching / editable**, not deletable — knight is one of several entities/flags/fixtures under test alongside doctrine, mission, filter, schema, health-scope, and API-parity coverage that must survive (e.g. `tests/unit/test_health.py` at 191 hits, `tests/e2e/test_mission_crud.py` at 67, `tests/e2e/test_filter_list.py` at 56, `tests/unit/test_models.py` at 44, `tests/unit/test_doctrine.py` at 40). |

